name w2-09-bingo
timeout 500
time 1981-06-07T12:55:00Z

# CAMPAIGN W2, SORTIE 9 OF 10 - OSIRAK 1981 (OPERATION OPERA): REACH, NOT COMBAT.
# THE MINIMUM-FUEL DECISION, ON THE EGRESS, WITH SOMEBODY BEHIND.
# Spec, anchor and sources: doc/campaigns/w2-osirak.md. Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
# CONTROL: THIS FILE CARRIES ITS OWN, INSIDE ITSELF. `w2bng1` and `w2bng2` are identical line for line
# except ONE: `w2bng1` declares `set brief_bingo_lbs 4000` and `w2bng2` does not. Same fuel state,
# same load, same spawn geometry, same task, same route. That is w4-07's shape (two MiG-29s one line
# apart) and it is why this sortie needs no sibling file.
#
# THE ANCHOR: the package flew "near the limit of its range" and the whole operation is defined by
# fuel [T4]. There is no documented bingo call and none is invented; what is declared here is a
# THRESHOLD, which is a briefing item and not a claim about 7 June 1981.
#
# THE ONE TACTICAL QUESTION, AND IT WAS DEAD CODE UNTIL 2026-07-30: DOES THE PILOT'S BINGO LOGIC
# ACTUALLY CHANGE A DECISION, OR IS IT A WARNING NOBODY ACTS ON? W3 measured the second answer and put
# a number on it - `FBPilot::CanPressOn` is the only line in the pilot that reads the BINGO warning, it
# sat behind a state that a more general branch overtook one tick after the defence window closed, and
# on `w3-06-bingo` the warning bit was set for 5,200 of 5,200 rows with an `eng_state` column
# byte-identical to a control with no declaration at all: SEVEN OF 184 COLUMNS AND ZERO METRES. The
# branch was re-ordered on 2026-07-30 (doc/pilot.md §7.4a) and the question is now REACHABLE. This
# sortie is where a campaign asks it.
#
# WHY THE FUEL LINE SITS WHERE IT SITS, because the answer depends on it: above it stand exactly two
# things - the defence (nobody stops defending because of fuel) and one's own round still needing its
# uplink (a shot already paid for is not thrown away) - and below it stands everything that is a
# statement about the PICTURE rather than about the AEROPLANE. So a jet at minimum fuel with a bandit
# on the nose still defends, and a jet at minimum fuel with a quiet scope goes home.
#
# WHAT `Abort` DOES AND WHAT IT DOES NOT, stated before the run because the verdict depends on it: the
# pilot turns COLD - 180 deg from the last thing that pointed at him - and STAYS there. It is NOT a
# route change: a fuel-driven return onto the landing waypoint is named in doc/pilot.md §Gaps and is
# not built. So `objective waypoints` on an aborting jet is expected to go UNMET, and that is the
# price of the decision rather than a defect. THE SPEC'S OWN VICTORY CONDITION FOR THIS MISSION
# ("waypoints home") IS THEREFORE NOT ACHIEVABLE and this file declares `survive` instead, with the
# waypoint left in the flight plan so the departure from it is visible in the telemetry.
#
# WHY 2 v 1 AND NOT THE SPEC'S 4 v 1: W1's saturation gate, 2026-07-30 - an air-to-air result above
# two aircraft a side is a FIXED POINT in this tree. Two is the boundary and this file sits on it.
#
# WHY THE DOME STANDS INTACT IN A FILE WHOSE SPEC SAYS "already hit" (`C21`). There is no declarable
# initial damage: a jet can be switched off, a ground object cannot be broken. The campaign layer
# could carry a destroyed object from an earlier sortie, but (a) this file is not a chain step - the
# campaign's one chain is 06 -> 10 - and (b) NO W2 SORTIE DESTROYS THE DOME ANYWAY, which is w2-01's
# own measured result. The object is therefore in the cast as an INTACT `target_hard` that nobody
# attacks, and the gap is written here rather than papered over.
#
# THE ARENA: the egress leg, the raid's inbound track flown backwards on 248.37 deg.
#   `w2bng1` f16    33.06756 44.12111 - 40 km west of the site, egressing, WITH the bingo declaration
#   `w2bng2` f16    33.05503 44.12704 - 1.5 km abeam, WITHOUT it. Everything else identical
#   `w2mig9` mig29  32.93511 43.72283 - 40 km AHEAD on the homeward leg, head-on, closing
#   `w2dom9` target_hard 33.20000 44.52000 - the reactor dome, intact, unattacked (see above)
#
# THE FUEL STATE, and it is derived rather than chosen: `set fuel_int_pct 40` = 2,788.8 lb of the
# model's own 6,972.0 lb internal capacity [DERIVED]. The threshold is `brief_bingo_lbs 4000`, above
# the fuel aboard, so the warning stands from the moment the entry reaches the DED - which is the same
# construction `bingo-abort.fbm` uses and the reason its abort fires at t = 4.1 s. The DED clamps any
# entry above 6,070 lb, so a campaign cannot declare a bingo higher than that.
#
# THE SIX STRUCTURAL DISCLOSURES, in short form (full text in w2-01-dome.fbm): `C5` half closed -
# THE TANK EXISTS AND AERIAL REFUELLING DOES NOT, which is exactly why this sortie exists: without a
# boom, minimum fuel is a decision and never a rendezvous, and what a tanker would have been worth
# here is the one thing this campaign cannot measure; `C20` open, `--elev const`, 0 m plane, real
# ground 42.83-1,599.22 m (MEASURED); `C10` level laydown - nothing is dropped here; `C7` no F-15;
# `FirstFlightKo`; W1's fixed point, handled by the 2v1 layout above. Fuel discipline:
# `set fuel_int_pct`, never `set fuel_pct`.
#
# READING RULE (binding): exit 3 is expected. Read, in this order: (1) the single
# `intercept BINGO_ABORT fuelLbs=... bingoLbs=... from=... haveTgt=...` line - or its absence,
# (2) the `eng_state` column of BOTH jets side by side: the whole result is the tick at which they
# stop being the same column, (3) each jet's heading track over the run, (4) `warn_active` (bit 2 =
# `FBWarnBingo`) on both, (5) `fuelLbs` in the last row. The comparison IS the measurement; neither
# jet alone proves anything.

# MEASURED (fingerprint c8c33b7a793ac8a1, exit 0): THE LINE IS ALIVE, AND IT COSTS THE JET ITS WAY
# HOME.
#   the log       `intercept BINGO_ABORT unit=w2bng1 t=4.1 fuelLbs=2773.81 bingoLbs=4000 from=closing
#                 haveTgt=1` - ONCE, and from `closing`, WITH a target. That is the strongest form of
#                 the branch W3 measured as dead code: the pilot is committed to an intercept and
#                 leaves it for fuel
#   eng_state     `w2bng1` search -> abort at t=4.1 s, terminal. `w2bng2`, the same jet 1.5 km abeam
#                 with the one line deleted, runs the engagement out and fires an AIM-120 at t=62.4 s
#   the tracks    the aborting jet turns cold and ends 199.2 km from its own home waypoint and 87.1 km BEYOND
#                 the target it was leaving - deeper into Iraq than it started; its control ends at 32.85/43.68, on its way home.
#                 Neither is shot down
#   warn_active   2 (`FBWarnBingo`) on `w2bng1` throughout, 0 on `w2bng2` - the only declared bit that
#                 differs between them
#   fuel          both 2,788.6 lb at t=0; 1,833.2 lb (abort) against 1,758.8 lb (press) at t=500. THE
#                 ABORTING JET DOES NOT EVEN SAVE FUEL over this window - it stays at 240 m while its
#                 control climbs to 390 m, and the altitude is worth more than the disengagement
#   THE PRICE OF THE DECISION, and it is a gap and not a defect: `Abort` is terminal and is NOT a
#   return to the landing waypoint (doc/pilot.md, Gaps). The spec's own victory condition for this
#   mission - "waypoints home" - is therefore not achievable, and this file declares `survive` and
#   says so instead of scoring a thing the pilot cannot do.

unit w2bng1
  module f16
  team friendly
  flight bng9 1
  spawn 33.06756 44.12111 240 248.4 450       # 40 km west of the site, outbound
  set gear up
  set fuel_int_pct 40                         # 2,788.8 lb internal - the limit-of-range state
  set datalink on
  set fcr_mode crm
  set rwr on
  set store 3 aim120
  set store 7 aim120
  set store 1 aim9
  set store 9 aim9
  set cmds_mode auto
  set cmds_program 1
  set cmds_chaff 60
  set cmds_flare 60
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set brief_bingo_lbs 4000                    # THE ONE LINE: above the fuel aboard, so BINGO stands
  set task intercept
  wp 32.80265 43.32515 240 450                # home, 120 km back down the track
  objective survive

unit w2bng2
  module f16
  team friendly
  flight bng9 2
  spawn 33.05503 44.12704 240 248.4 450       # 1.5 km abeam
  set gear up
  set fuel_int_pct 40
  set datalink on
  set fcr_mode crm
  set rwr on
  set store 3 aim120
  set store 7 aim120
  set store 1 aim9
  set store 9 aim9
  set cmds_mode auto
  set cmds_program 1
  set cmds_chaff 60
  set cmds_flare 60
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task intercept
  wp 32.80265 43.32515 240 450
  objective survive

unit w2mig9
  module mig29
  team hostile
  spawn 32.93511 43.72283 3000 68.4 480       # 40 km ahead on the homeward leg, head-on
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 70
  set n019_mode rad
  set n019_emission illum
  set rwr on
  set store 3 r27r
  set store 7 r27r
  set store 5 r73
  set store 6 r73
  set cmds_mode auto
  set cmds_program 1
  set cmds_chaff 30
  set cmds_flare 30
  set brief_master_arm arm
  set task intercept
  wp 33.20000 44.52000 3000 480
  objective survive

unit w2dom9
  module target_hard
  team hostile
  spawn 33.20000 44.52000 ground 68.4 0
